# Attack-path analysis
Final: ignore | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Although the finding is valid as a product bug, it should be ignored for security criticality. The affected code is in scope for the runtime, but the data flow is a hard-coded frontend help URL at src/frontend/src/main.js:1974 through api.OpenURL at src/frontend/src/main.js:1022-1025 into App.OpenURL at src/app.go:248-253. The backend allowlist at src/app.go:263-273 blocks feedback.vrchat.com because it is absent from the approved host list. That is a failure to open intended documentation, not an attacker-controlled open redirect or allowlist bypass. There is no public ingress, no service account or cloud identity, no secrets involved, and no security impact beyond user inconvenience.
## Likelihood
ignore - The broken behavior is easy for a user to trigger, but it is not exploitable by an attacker in a security-relevant way because the URL is hard-coded and the backend rejects rather than opens an untrusted destination.
## Impact
ignore - The only demonstrated impact is a blocked help link. There is no data exposure, privilege escalation, arbitrary URL opening, SSRF, code execution, persistence, or compromise of Discord webhook secrets.
## Assumptions
- Analysis is limited to repository artifacts in /workspace/ClipForVRChat and does not call cloud APIs.
- The Wails desktop application is the main shipped runtime component; the Vite localhost development server is development-only unless explicitly run by a developer.
- The reported behavior is evaluated as an external URL allowlist mismatch, not as a bypass of the allowlist.
- A user runs the desktop application
- The user navigates to the auto-capture guidance UI
- The user clicks the VRChat Feedback help button
## Path
User click
  -> Vue openURL('https://feedback.vrchat.com/...')
  -> api.OpenURL / App.OpenURL
  -> trustedExternalURL allowlist check
  -> feedback.vrchat.com not allowed
  -> help link fails to open
## Path evidence
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:1974` - The UI button passes a hard-coded https://feedback.vrchat.com help URL to openURL.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:1022-1025` - The frontend openURL method logs the click and calls the Wails api.OpenURL bridge without any security-sensitive transformation.
- `src/app.go:248-253` - The backend App.OpenURL validates the URL using trustedExternalURL before calling runtime.BrowserOpenURL.
- `src/app.go:263-273` - trustedExternalURL enforces HTTPS and only allowlists github.com, hatolife.booth.pm, support.discord.com, and x.com, excluding feedback.vrchat.com.
- `src/main.go:213-230` - The application is a Wails desktop app with embedded assets and a bound local App object, not a public network service.
- `src/wails.json:1-12` - Repository configuration identifies ClipForVRChat as a Wails application; the localhost Vite URL is a development server setting.
## Narrative
Static review confirms a real functional bug: the frontend added a hard-coded VRChat Feedback help button, but the backend OpenURL policy only permits github.com, hatolife.booth.pm, support.discord.com, and x.com. The validation evidence also showed the exact URL reaches the rejection branch. This is not a security vulnerability because there is no attacker-controlled URL, no public service exposure, no bypass of the allowlist, no secret access, no code execution, and no cross-boundary impact. The only proven impact is that a local user cannot open the intended help page from the application.
## Controls
- HTTPS-only external URL validation
- Explicit trustedExternalURL host allowlist
- Wails desktop app local bridge rather than public ingress
- Vue template escaping for rendered UI content
- BrowserOpenURL reached only after backend policy validation
## Blindspots
- Static analysis cannot confirm the exact user-visible UI error behavior on a real Windows Wails/WebView2 runtime.
- No dynamic end-to-end GUI execution was performed in this attack-path stage.
- Repository review cannot prove whether future releases or external packaging modify the allowlist behavior.